<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Hi Shepherd,<br><br>What do you mean with "the speed doesn't slow down too much"?<br>Speed of what?<br>What do you expect to see? And what do you really see?<br><br>The way, I have realized double precision has affect to quality of simulation, that my Hexapod was shaking/jittering after I have reinstalled BulletPhysics, with the same simulation values as before, and I knew that simulation was more stable last time. I forgot to turn on double precision. So I did it. Quality of simulation was back.<br>So try to build some chain of constraints, fix it in the space, set some of my values (above) and you should see different type of behavior.<br><br>God, I have to do something with my english.<br>Hope this helps.</div></blockquote>Don't worry too much about your English, you have expressed yourself perfectly well.<br><br>Then you discovered this by practice. What I mean is that by using double precision, the speed is expected to be about 2x slower than single float mode. So there should be something, like simulation steps, CPU usage etc to see this difference. <br><br>Cheers.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=7605">Dr.Shepherd</a> — Sat Mar 23, 2013 9:32 pm</p><hr />
